net.wireguard
  Run a WireGuard interface, for a private path to SSH
  Does not apply to this host: whether the kernel has WireGuard is unknown:
  /sys/module/wireguard is missing and modprobe could not tell.

Why
  A server reachable only through a private network has no SSH port for the
  internet to scan or brute force. WireGuard gives this host a tunnel address
  that only your devices' keys can reach, with no outside service;
  firewall.inbound can then close public SSH. WireGuard does not answer packets
  without a valid handshake, so its port reveals nothing.

Params (in the module's profile table)
  port (default 51820)
      The UDP port WireGuard listens on; firewall.inbound accepts it.
  address (default "")
      This server's tunnel address and subnet, e.g. "10.86.12.1/24" (IPv4, a
      prefix of 8 to 24). Empty: a /24 in 10.0.0.0/8 derived from
      /etc/machine-id, so each server gets its own. Changing it on a running wg0
      is refused.
  endpoint (default "")
      The address or host name clients connect to, only for the printed client
      config. Empty: guessed from this host's public address.
  peers (default [])
      Your devices: { name = "laptop", public_key = "<from wg pubkey>", host = 2
      }. `host` (2 to 254) is the last part of the peer's tunnel address. Public
      keys only: a device's private key stays on it.

Fixes these audit controls
  NET-008 [medium] WireGuard private keys readable only by root
